+## Server Session And Confirmed Context
+
+Inspect the actual host auth endpoints, cookie policy, CSRF/CORS/origin gates,
+Core's `EnterpriseRuntimeContextService`, its provider/options and focused specs,
+and the consumer's HTTP chain before implementing authentication. A cookie session
+is a host contract, not a new identity API in a Praxis component. In the API
+Quickstart, `/auth/login` accepts username/password and returns 204 with HttpOnly
+SESSION; `/auth/session` verifies acceptance and current validity; `/auth/logout`
+revokes the session. Keep credentials out of assets, browser storage and logs.
+
+Separate session authentication from corporate runtime context. Register
+`providePraxisEnterpriseRuntimeContext` only when that host declares the integration;
+a root-provided service alone does not make it mandatory. The Quickstart's corporate
+adapter is deliberately not auto-composed; Config's default returns
+`RUNTIME_PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE` rather than fabricating READY. A verified
+`/auth/session` 204 can remain active when an optional context probe is unavailable.
+Record that limit and keep context-dependent personalization disabled; operation
+authorization still comes from current server session/grants and fresh discovery.
+Do not activate a new grant directory/provider or create a simplified second context
+just to make the existing standalone bulk path work.
+
+Consume the actual server snapshot instead of a persona or locally stored user/tenant.
+Bootstrap without contextual claims; `effectiveContext` and its headers are usable
+only after a ready snapshot. Use
+opaque context choices, `expectedSelectionVersion` and all published cursor pages;
+do not manufacture choice references or infer a tenant from the first option.
+When a fetch path needs `PAX_FETCH_HEADERS`, project confirmed context through Core's
+header methods and keep it symmetric with the HTTP interceptor. The global factory
+alone cannot restrict destination origin; audit each transport before attaching it.
+
+Restrict cookie credentials and contextual headers to the configured API origin and
+its declared paths. Preserve browser Origin in a local proxy, verify upstream TLS,
+and forward auth endpoints when that host uses them. Only the official trusted-proxy
+policy may authorize forwarded headers; proxy headers must not inject credentials or
+identity. Same-origin Angular XSRF reads the readable CSRF cookie and echoes its
+header. Cross-origin `withCredentials` does not provide that echo or make the API's
+cookie readable by the host. Verify the deployment's effective policy; do not disable
+CSRF, rewrite Origin, or relax CORS to make a client work.
+
+Treat login/context/logout as projection transitions. Suspend obsolete routed content
+while changing authentication, invalidate on a current session-expiry response, and use a revision/epoch
+so an old response cannot revoke or restore a newer session. A successful login needs
+a session readback before accepting identity. Failed logout is uncertain: retain a
+blocked projection and expose verify-session/retry-logout until the server confirms
+absence or confirms the actual active session/context. A transport error is not logout
+proof. Do not remount a page under an old cookie during that transition. Once corporate
+context was confirmed, preserve that history in the host session service across
+consumer remounts. Loss blocks corporate projections until ready is reconfirmed or
+the server confirms session absence/revocation. Starting a login attempt or receiving
+401 for rejected login credentials does not prove an existing cookie was revoked;
+failed replacement authentication must not erase that history or unblock old-cookie
+projections. Keep the existing options/history contract instead of adding a compatibility
+flag. When context never existed, optional 503/null must not turn valid SESSION into
+anonymous or synthesize contextual headers.
+
+Bind persistence identity such as Page Builder's `pageIdentity` to confirmed tenant
+and user. Until it exists, disable user customization/auto-persistence rather than
+saving under an invented visitor/demo identity. Resource availability remains governed
+by current metadata/capabilities/actions; a host route, account label or selected
+context never grants an operation.
+
+Prove trusted versus foreign origin, accepted versus blocked cookies, current and
+late 401, logout success/uncertainty, context invalidation and paginated choices in
+focal host specs. Then prove browser cookie/CORS/CSRF behavior with the actual official
+origin; HTTP mocks cannot certify it. For bulk integration use the native Core/Table/
+CRUD path and real published bindings. Protected READ may be delegated by the backend
+to a different authorized requester; it never substitutes for creator-bound confirmation. Remove fictitious containment/reajuste actions
+when the backend has no matching contract; do not replace mocks with guessed URLs.
